---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Playground from '../islands/Playground.vue'
---
<Layout
  title="Playground — Etassia"
  description="Edit diagram source and watch the Etassia WebAssembly engine render it to SVG in the browser."
>
  <article class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-14">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="block-label">The bench</p>
      <h1 class="display mt-3 text-4xl leading-tight">Render diagram source in the browser</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft dark:text-ink-soft-dark">
        Rendering a diagram is the work of a rendering engine, not of a visual impression, and the
        playground exists so that the engine can be exercised directly. The page runs the same
        WebAssembly build of the Rust runtime that the npm package ships, and that build carries
        the Mermaid family of the engine; the source in the editor is handed to the engine over
        the same boundary the bindings use, and the engine returns the SVG, which the page
        inserts without modification. The output re-renders 250 milliseconds after the last
        keystroke.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft dark:text-ink-soft-dark">
        The boundary of the browser build is the boundary of its native renderer set. A source
        whose diagram type has no native renderer in the browser build reports the Unported
        condition in the output panel, together with a statement of what a runtime binding does
        with it; the full list of native and unported types is on the
        <a class="text-pen dark:text-pen-dark underline underline-offset-2" href="/syntax">syntax page</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <Playground client:only="vue" />
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
